  so that pandemic weary canadians have  some place safe to play  heather your ex-west global news  kananaskis alberta  still ahead the budget cuts that set off  theory in guatemala  a wave of anger and unrest in guatemala  tonight  thousands of protesters took to the  streets after the government passed a  new budget  that will cut spending for health care  and education some demonstrators broke  into congress and set one part of the  building on fire  they say the budget was negotiated in  secret while the public was distracted  by back-to-back hurricanes  and the pandemic ethiopia's prime  minister  abi ahmed is giving rebel leaders in  tigray 72 hours to surrender peacefully  before troops and tanks surround the  region's capital  ethiopian forces are preparing a major  assault against the tigray people's  liberation front  if they don't lay down their arms the  military is warning civilians caught in  the crossfire to save themselves now  because there will be no mercy for the  rebel leaders who are accused of hiding  among the general population  more than 36 thousand refugees have fled  tigre into neighboring sudan  since the conflict broke out earlier  this month  ethnic armenians in nagorno-karabakh  paid respects to the fallen today as  they pick up the pieces following a  six-week war  hundreds possibly thousands of lives  were lost after heavy fighting between  armenia and azerbaijan in the breakaway  region  the enclave is situated within  azerbaijan's borders  but is largely populated by ethnic  armenians  a russia brokered ceasefire saw  azerbaijan take back control of  territory it seized during the conflict  today russian peacekeepers transported  drinking water and other aid  to residents in remote parts of the  region as part of the agreement to end  the hostilities  russia deployed about 2 000 troops to  ensure the truce  holds up next indigenous representation  with a hollywood powerhouse we'll  explain when global national returns  it's not every day you get to work with  one of hollywood's most powerful players  but a small production company  has teamed up with disney to shed light  on indigenous youth  the company's ceo jacob pratt comes from  a saskatchewan first nation  now he's seizing a rare opportunity for  indigenous representation  within the entertainment industry brady  ratzlaff explains  jacob pratt loves to tell stories  through the lens of a camera hi  his team at skoda entertainment in  california recently signed a big deal  with an even bigger company  we were just getting up and running and  we landed disney as our first major  client  that deal provided the platform for  pratt and his team to create  three mini episodes featuring indigenous  youth  one way that we make a difference is  through our art as part of native  american heritage month which is  celebrated in the u.s  every november we wanted to share each  one of those stories because they're so  different  but also they're  all advocates for indigenous cultures  and  social justice pratt was born on the  cote first nation in saskatchewan  but grew up in the george gordon first  nation he completed his business degree  in regina  before making the move to los angeles to  pursue a career in the entertainment  industry  pratt says disney is working to move  away from its past reputation of  stereotyping some cultures  and this new deal is a way to bring more  indigenous material to the table that  portrays his people in an authentic way  they've come a long ways as an  organization  and have acknowledged their shortcomings  of in the past  and have been actively trying to um  bring more diversity to their content  which is great  for everybody he says the episodes are  part of a series called use your voice  for young people that are making  differences and  trying to make a change within their  community are being featured  and their stories are being shared  members of his team say it's pratt's  attention to detail and passion 